WEBVTT

00:01.500 --> 00:17.500
Hi everybody, it is the source
code for Friday, August 27th,
2004.

00:17.500 --> 00:20.500
Good afternoon or good morning
or good evening, wherever you
are, wherever you're listening

00:20.500 --> 00:25.500
to this, either you clicked on
the link on the website or you
might be subscribing to

00:25.500 --> 00:26.500
it through RSS.

00:26.500 --> 00:28.500
Thank you very much for
subscribing.

00:29.500 --> 00:34.500
I've been thinking a lot about
what we need to do with this
show in particular

00:34.500 --> 00:38.500
because I'm even getting pretty
bored of talking about myself.

00:38.500 --> 00:43.500
And then last night it hit me as
I was trying to get to sleep
around, I had to go to bed

00:43.500 --> 00:50.500
early, only two more days,
Monday and Tuesday, the last two
days of the morning show.

00:50.500 --> 00:57.500
And I was thinking that really
what maybe at this audio blog or
audio show can be about,

00:57.500 --> 01:00.500
maybe it can just be really for
users and developers to bring
them together.

01:00.500 --> 01:02.500
I mean, I'm...

01:03.000 --> 01:08.000
100% a user on all sides of the
broadcast equation.

01:08.000 --> 01:11.000
I really like receiving stuff
and I like making stuff,

01:11.000 --> 01:13.000
sending it out as well.

01:13.000 --> 01:16.000
And I think that so far we've
been pretty successful at

01:16.000 --> 01:21.000
bringing together a number of
developers to work on the

01:21.000 --> 01:24.000
iPod platform and get stuff
working.

01:24.000 --> 01:28.000
And these may not even be
developers per se.

01:28.000 --> 01:32.000
These could be just users who
have a little more

01:32.000 --> 01:35.000
knowledge and experience at
making the computers do things

01:35.000 --> 01:36.000
that they want them to do.

01:36.000 --> 01:39.000
So I'm going to see if at all we
can make this a

01:39.000 --> 01:42.000
platform of its own for users
and developers.

01:42.000 --> 01:46.000
And if you have a comment,
obviously you can leave a

01:46.000 --> 01:48.000
comment on the web blog or send
me an email

01:48.000 --> 01:49.000
adamantcurry.com.

01:49.000 --> 01:53.000
But maybe if you have something
to contribute, you can

01:53.000 --> 01:55.000
create your own audio blog post.

01:55.000 --> 01:58.000
Send me a URL point to it and
I'll pick it up.

01:58.000 --> 02:01.000
And then maybe we can share some
of your ideas or

02:01.000 --> 02:04.000
questions or stuff that you're
looking for or things that we

02:04.500 --> 02:08.500
need to make our computers do
and how we can connect them all
together through the internet.

02:08.500 --> 02:15.500
And I made a very important
discovery yesterday, although
the application wiretap for Mac
OS

02:15.500 --> 02:20.500
10 had been pointed out to me a
couple of times by people in my
quest for getting the right

02:20.500 --> 02:25.500
audio software to record these
with.

02:25.500 --> 02:29.500
The interface really, I had
downloaded it, I think the
interface just confused me so

02:29.500 --> 02:34.500
much. I didn't realize that it
does exactly what I wanted to
do, namely record all the

02:34.500 --> 02:40.500
audio that would normally come
out of my speaker. I want that
recorded digitally just by
listening

02:40.500 --> 02:45.500
to the core audio bits or
whatever it is. And that's
exactly what wiretap does. I
didn't

02:45.500 --> 02:53.500
think it did because when I
looked at the preferences file
and I think it's selected by
default,

02:53.500 --> 02:57.500
it says save files to and then
you give it a place to save to
and then it says

02:57.500 --> 03:03.500
you can add prefix if you want,
which can be the aim. And so
when I clicked on the
application

03:03.500 --> 03:05.500
name it seemed only to want to
save files.

03:06.000 --> 03:10.000
save files with the application
name that was at the front that
was recording.

03:10.000 --> 03:14.000
So I figured well it only
records one application at a
time.

03:14.000 --> 03:23.000
And then yesterday while I was
doing the source code, Cindy
Camilleri came online on iChat

03:23.000 --> 03:28.000
and since she doesn't come
online when usually when I am
because we are in different,
she's

03:28.000 --> 03:32.000
in New York and I'm in obviously
in Belgium.

03:32.000 --> 03:38.000
And I always have iChat saying
you know, Skipper is here which
is her nickname, Skipper.

03:38.000 --> 03:45.000
And I heard the iChat alert that
she was online while I was
recording and I didn't think
that

03:45.000 --> 03:46.000
you'd hear it.

03:46.000 --> 03:50.000
But then I listened back to the
yesterday show, yep sure enough
it was there.

03:50.000 --> 03:54.000
So I started experimenting and
indeed it records everything.

03:54.000 --> 03:59.000
So instead of a mixing console
now in front of me, I basically
have all my applications

03:59.000 --> 04:04.000
open that I normally use to play
bits of audio or use in an audio
show like this.

04:04.000 --> 04:07.000
I have three quick time windows
open.

04:07.500 --> 04:14.500
the Rachmaninoff remix which is,
well, by default now become the
tune for source code.

04:14.500 --> 04:18.500
I have a song of the day all
queued up and also I have iTunes
standing by in case I think

04:18.500 --> 04:22.500
of something I need to search
and iTunes is great for that,
just typing your search

04:22.500 --> 04:25.500
and immediately you get your
results.

04:25.500 --> 04:32.500
Okay, before I go on to with
more about what source code
could be and a little update

04:32.500 --> 04:37.500
on iPodder, here's something
that came in through my own
iPodder, at least I'm using

04:37.500 --> 04:46.500
the get enclosure.pearl script
and this came in from, what was
this, blog digger

04:46.500 --> 04:51.500
and this is from their their
media RSS file and apparently
some German guy has

04:51.500 --> 04:57.500
written a book called blogs and
his name is Don Alfonso and here
is an audio

04:57.500 --> 05:01.500
show that was out there on the
internet somewhere and I just
want to play a

05:01.500 --> 05:04.500
little bit of it because it's
kind of cool to hear Germans
talk about web logs.

05:04.500 --> 05:08.500
15.40, you can hear the exact
same thing in Germany.

05:09.000 --> 05:11.000
These texts are only available
on the Internet.

05:11.000 --> 05:16.000
The book is a special logbook
for the reader.

05:16.000 --> 05:21.000
It shows a new world and often
also a radical point of view.

05:21.000 --> 05:23.000
The book is about blogs.

05:23.000 --> 05:28.000
The title of the book is also
published in the beginning of
September in the book of
Schwarzkopf and Schwarzkopf.

05:28.000 --> 05:32.000
A book published by Don Alfonso
and Kai...

05:32.000 --> 05:34.000
What's the name again?

05:34.000 --> 05:35.000
Don Alfonso?

05:35.000 --> 05:36.000
Kai-Pal.

05:36.000 --> 05:37.000
Exactly.

05:37.000 --> 05:38.000
Kai-Pal.

05:38.000 --> 05:39.000
The book is about blogs.

05:39.000 --> 05:45.000
16 webloggers write about text
and form on the Internet and why
they take over the Internet.

05:45.000 --> 05:46.000
I love it.

05:46.000 --> 05:49.000
Okay, so if you read German
there's a book called Blogs.

05:49.000 --> 05:51.000
Blogs is out now in Germany.

05:51.000 --> 06:01.000
Okay, so about the source code,
I'd like to see this turn into
some form of a platform

06:01.000 --> 06:07.000
where users and developers can
talk about how we can work
together and make our collective
lives better.

06:07.000 --> 06:10.000
I know one thing for sure that
all of the...

06:10.500 --> 06:15.500
users I know, if they are using
shareware and they use it
consistently or if there's a
registration

06:15.500 --> 06:16.500
fee.

06:16.500 --> 06:22.500
Most everyone always says the
same thing, hey, I want to pay
my dues and make sure people

06:22.500 --> 06:23.500
get paid.

06:23.500 --> 06:24.500
So I think there is a market
there.

06:24.500 --> 06:30.500
It's probably on a per user
basis, small license fees, but
there's a lot of software to be

06:30.500 --> 06:38.500
made and if we can aggregate the
ideas when it comes to per
topic, obviously, then

06:38.500 --> 06:44.500
maybe we can get some economies
of scale going like has happened
with iPodder.

06:44.500 --> 06:50.500
So the history real quick, I
created this script, an Apple
script that takes a list of

06:50.500 --> 06:57.500
RSS feeds with MP3 enclosures,
downloads those and contrary to
popular belief, by the way,

06:57.500 --> 07:00.500
it does check to see if the file
has already been downloaded.

07:00.500 --> 07:04.500
It doesn't download again unless
the file has been removed
because I use the curl

07:04.500 --> 07:07.500
minus F switch.

07:07.500 --> 07:11.500
It downloads them and then it
transfers them to your iTunes,
which if you have a

07:12.000 --> 07:18.000
iPod automatically syncs up to
your iPod and in iTunes it
actually creates a playlist
which

07:18.000 --> 07:24.000
is equal or equivalent to the
name of the channel that or the
RSS feed that you're
downloading.

07:24.000 --> 07:27.000
It takes all that information
from the RSS feed.

07:27.000 --> 07:29.000
So a couple different versions
showed up.

07:29.000 --> 07:35.000
We have a Pearl version which
I'm actually using myself now
and there is now a Windows

07:35.000 --> 07:39.000
version and apparently there's
nothing extra to install, no
bits that you need.

07:39.000 --> 07:44.000
So I'm going to just download
the script and it's good to go.

07:44.000 --> 07:48.000
It's made by Peter Overbeke and
I'm pretty sure he's Dutch.

07:48.000 --> 07:51.000
I haven't met him other than
online.

07:51.000 --> 07:53.000
I've linked to him today on my
web blog.

07:53.000 --> 08:00.000
It's opportunities.nl slash
wordpress and he even sent me a
ping about it because he's

08:00.000 --> 08:05.000
up to, he's done like three
versions now and it's really
ready to be tested.

08:05.000 --> 08:09.000
So please give Peter's script a
test because if we can get this
working for Windows, and

08:09.000 --> 08:13.000
by the way it works with the
iTunes, the Windows version of
iTunes.

08:13.500 --> 08:21.500
If we can get this working, then
this concept of an iPod
platform, meaning all mp3
devices,

08:21.500 --> 08:28.500
that can download stuff without
you doing anything and just have
shows ready for you, I think

08:28.500 --> 08:34.500
we're just on the verge of a
huge potential being unleashed
here.

08:34.500 --> 08:39.500
So again, if you could, and I've
linked to it on my web blog,
check out the iPodder

08:39.500 --> 08:44.500
for Windows and let's say get
some feedback on how it's doing
and if it's working and if it's

08:44.500 --> 08:49.500
performing. And then of course,
the next step, which is needed
for both platforms, both for

08:49.500 --> 08:56.500
Windows and the Mac, and for
Linux and anything else that we
can find that people have this
desire,

08:56.500 --> 09:07.500
is getting the shows, the mp3s
from iTunes to non-ipod mp3
players. And I have a feeling

09:07.500 --> 09:13.500
that's going to be a little bit
more challenging. So just about
at the end for today, yes, that
was

09:13.500 --> 09:16.500
really long time.

09:15.000 --> 09:19.000
them. As I said, I'm trying to
keep these around 15 minutes,
which is, you know, just
believing

09:19.000 --> 09:22.000
in Andy Warhol's vision.

09:22.000 --> 09:31.000
Today, 14 years ago, Patricia
turned to me and said, we need
to get this child up. And

09:31.000 --> 09:37.000
we knew that we were going to
have a C-section way ahead of
time because Christina was a

09:37.000 --> 09:42.000
breach. And we went to the
hospital and Dr. Ladosi was
there and Dr. Sony, the wife

09:42.000 --> 09:49.000
Sony with an eye at the
hospital, St. Barnabas in New
Jersey. And they said, okay,

09:49.000 --> 09:52.000
you know, we're gonna have this
kid today. And it was around
seven o'clock and it was

09:52.000 --> 09:59.000
a really great vibe in the
operating room. And as the
doctor literally picked

09:59.000 --> 10:05.000
Christina up out of Patricia, it
was just an amazing sight. On
the radio, it

10:05.000 --> 10:10.000
was Light FM was on and the
Beatles in my life started to
play. And it was

10:10.000 --> 10:15.000
something that we will never,
ever forget. Now, of course, it
would be easy for me

10:15.000 --> 10:16.000
to play.

10:16.500 --> 10:19.500
the Beatles right now and we'd
all have an emotional moment.

10:19.500 --> 10:26.500
But since my daughter,
Christina, turns 14 today, I
figure I'd honor her since she
does

10:26.500 --> 10:30.500
share the bar slash recording
studio slash computer room with
me.

10:30.500 --> 10:35.500
I'd honor her by playing the
brand new song by her favorite
band.

10:35.500 --> 10:39.500
And of course, I need to remind
you that you do have to vote for
them for the viewers'

10:39.500 --> 10:45.500
